module

public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Module.OppositeOfCommSemiring
public import StacksFrontier.Deformation.Examples.RingsTangentSpace
public import StacksFrontier.Deformation.SquareZero.ExtensionPushoutPullback

/-!
# First-order deformations of an algebra as square-zero extensions

Let `k` be a field, `P` a `k`-algebra, `V` a `k`-vector space and `k ⊕ V` the square-zero
extension of `k` by `V` of Stacks 06IB. A *first-order flat deformation* of `P` over `k ⊕ V`
is a flat `k ⊕ V`-algebra `Q` together with a surjection `π : Q → P` of `k`-algebras which is
compatible with the augmentation `k ⊕ V → k` and whose kernel is the ideal generated by the
augmentation ideal.

This file turns such a deformation into a square-zero extension of the `k`-algebra `P` by the
`P`-module `P ⊗[k] V`, which is the shape in which Stacks 0GPT classifies it by
`Ext¹_P(NL_{P/k}, P ⊗[k] V)`, and it records the two comparison statements the identification
of the tangent space needs:

* a `k`-algebra map of two such deformations which is compatible with the reductions is the
  same thing as a map of the associated extensions over a `k`-linear map `V → V'`, so the
  extension class is natural in `V` (through **Stacks 0GPV**); and
* an isomorphism of the associated extensions is automatically an isomorphism of the
  deformations, because the action of the augmentation ideal on `Q` is recoverable from the
  reduction and the identification of the kernel.

## Main definitions

* `Deformation.Examples.firstOrderExtension`: the square-zero extension of `P` by `P ⊗[k] V`
  attached to a first-order flat deformation `Q` of `P` over `k ⊕ V`.
* `Deformation.Examples.tensorRightMap`: the `P`-linear coefficient map
  `P ⊗[k] V → P ⊗[k] V'` induced by a `k`-linear map `V → V'`.

## Main results

* `Deformation.Examples.sq_zero_ker_of_isFirstOrderReduction`: the kernel of the reduction of a
  first-order deformation has square zero.
* `Deformation.Examples.kernelModule_smul` and
  `Deformation.Examples.coe_kernelModule_smul`: the `P`-action on that kernel is multiplication
  by an arbitrary lift.
* `Deformation.Examples.firstOrderExtension_toB` and
  `Deformation.Examples.kernelInclusion_firstOrderExtension`: the reduction and the kernel
  inclusion of that extension are the reduction of `Q` and the canonical map of
  `Deformation.Examples.firstOrderConormalHom`.
* `Deformation.Examples.map_firstOrderConormalHom`: a reduction-compatible `k`-algebra map of
  deformations which is semilinear over `f : V → V'` carries the canonical map of the source
  to that of the target along `P ⊗ f`. Its converse
  `Deformation.Examples.inr_smul_of_map_firstOrderConormalHom` recovers semilinearity from
  that compatibility.
* `Deformation.Examples.extClass_firstOrderExtension`: the class of the extension attached to
  the target is the image of the class attached to the source under
  `Ext¹_P(NL_{P/k}, P ⊗[k] V) → Ext¹_P(NL_{P/k}, P ⊗[k] V')`. This is the naturality in `V`
  of the extension class of a first-order deformation.
* `Deformation.Examples.smul_of_compatible_algHom`: a map of the associated square-zero
  extensions is automatically `k ⊕ V`-linear, hence a map of deformations.
* `Stacks.Deformation.naiveCotangentExtOneMap_comp`: the map on first Ext induced by a
  composite of coefficient maps is the composite of the induced maps.

## Implementation notes

The surjectivity of `Q → P` and the description of its kernel as the ideal generated by the
augmentation ideal are *hypotheses* here rather than consequences of a pushout square. In the
deformation category of Stacks 0DY1 they come from the pushout condition on a morphism, by
`ker_algebraMap_eq_map_ker_of_pushout` of
`StacksFrontier/Deformation/Examples/Geometric/Rings.lean` and `isPushout_quotientMap`, which is
`private` in `StacksFrontier/Deformation/Examples/RingsUnobstructed.lean`; the statements below
take the two conclusions directly. Nothing here depends on the deformation category, which is
why the file is purely commutative-algebraic.

The annihilation lemma `Deformation.Examples.kerIdeal_smul_eq_zero` is imported from
`StacksFrontier/Deformation/Examples/RingsTangentSpace.lean`; the two multiplication lemmas
below it, which turn that submodule into an ideal of square zero, are proved here.

The `P`-module structure on the kernel of `Q → P` is `Deformation.Examples.kernelModule`,
obtained there by descending the `Q`-action without choosing a section. Its scalar action is
therefore not given by a formula, and `Deformation.Examples.kernelModule_smul` is the lemma
that computes it by an arbitrary lift; every proof below that touches the `P`-action on the
kernel goes through it.

The square-zero extension problem of Stacks 0GPT confines the middle ring of an extension of
`P` by `N` to the universe `max uP uN`, so all of `k`, `P`, `Q` and `V` live in one universe
here, as they do in `StacksFrontier/Deformation/SquareZero/ExtensionPushoutPullback.lean`.

Nothing here carries the tag of Stacks 0DY3, whose tangent-space clause is
`T𝓓_P = Ext¹_P(NL_{P/k}, P)`; the infinitesimal-automorphism clause of that tag is
`Deformation.Examples.exists_infinitesimalAutomorphismGroupMulEquivDerivation` of
`StacksFrontier/Deformation/Examples/Geometric/RingsInfinitesimalAutomorphisms.lean`. What this
file supplies is the commutative algebra that clause needs, in the form in which the
deformation category consumes it; what is still owed is the dictionary between the fibres of
`Deformation.Examples.ringDeformationProjection` over the Artinian square-zero extensions and
the ring data above, in both directions — in particular the construction of a flat
`k ⊕ V`-algebra out of an arbitrary square-zero extension of `P` by `P ⊗[k] V`, where the
flatness comes from Stacks 063Y through `Ideal.bijective_tensorSMulHomToKer_iff`.

The identification cannot be obtained as a `k`-linear equivalence with the present
infrastructure, and the obstruction is worth recording. The canonical vector-space structure of
Stacks 06IH on the tangent space is defined through the whole functor `V ↦ 𝓕(k[V])`, so
linearity of a map out of it is only available from
`Deformation.finiteDimensional_artinianTangentSpace_of_natural_injection`, which asks for a
receptacle functor that is `Functor.Linear k`. For the receptacle
`V ↦ Ext¹_P(NL_{P/k}, P ⊗[k] V)` with
`Stacks.Deformation.naiveCotangentExtOneMap` as its action on maps, that amounts to
`naiveCotangentExtOneMap A B (b • c) = b • naiveCotangentExtOneMap A B c`, which unfolds to
`B`-linearity of `Stacks.Deformation.extCokernelIso`. That comparison factors through
`NaiveCotangent.extOne_naiveCotangentComplex_addEquiv_cokernel`, which is
`Classical.choice` applied to a `Nonempty (… ≃+ …)`, so no property of it beyond additivity is
provable; and the finite-dimensionality of
`Deformation.Examples.finiteDimensional_naiveCotangentExtOne` is stated for the Ext group with
its own `Module P` structure, so it does not transport to the conormal-cokernel presentation
`Stacks.Deformation.extOneCokernel` along a merely additive equivalence either. Closing the
tangent-space clause of Stacks 0DY3 as a linear equivalence, and with it Stacks 0ET4, therefore
needs that comparison replaced by a characterized one.

